# Updating `devguard-seed-data.sql`

The nix tests run against a seeded DevGuard database. Whenever the schema or the
test fixtures change, the seed dump has to be regenerated from a clean DevGuard
instance.

## 1. Start a clean DevGuard on latest `main`

Make sure both repositories are on the latest `main`.

Frontend (devguard-web):

```bash
npm run dev
```

Backend (devguard):

```bash
make clean
make migrate
make
```

## 2. Create the test data in the web UI

1. Register a new account (any credentials will do).
2. Create an organization named `testorg`.
3. Inside it, create a group named `testgroup`.
4. Inside that group, create a repository named `testrepo`.

## 3. Create an access token

Go to the repository settings of `testrepo` and create a personal access token
with the `scan` and `manage` scopes and a long expiry date.

Copy the generated private key and update the `token` value in
`nix-tests.yml`.

## 4. Dump the seed data

Run this in the documentation repository:

```bash
docker exec devguard-postgresql-1 pg_dump \
  --data-only \
  --disable-triggers \
  --no-owner --no-privileges \
  --schema=public \
  --exclude-table-data='public.cves' \
  --exclude-table-data='public.cve_affected_component' \
  --exclude-table-data='public.cve_relationships' \
  --exclude-table-data='public.affected_components' \
  --exclude-table-data='public.malicious_packages' \
  --exclude-table-data='public.malicious_affected_components' \
  -U devguard -d devguard \
  > src/nix-tests/devguard-seed-data.sql
```

The large vulnerability tables (CVEs, affected components, malicious packages)
are excluded on purpose — they are populated by DevGuard itself and would blow
up the dump.

## 5. Push

Commit and push the updated `devguard-seed-data.sql` and `nix-tests.yml`. The
tests are then back on the latest state.
